WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.512
The principle is that
deciding isn't doing.

2
00:00:02.512 --> 00:00:06.709
Approvers don't make changes, the
people who make changes don't approve,

3
00:00:06.709 --> 00:00:08.951
and nobody approves their own access.

4
00:00:08.951 --> 00:00:12.175
Here's the catch: Entra
doesn't enforce any of that.

5
00:00:12.175 --> 00:00:15.052
Application Administrator can grant most

6
00:00:15.052 --> 00:00:17.850
kinds of consent and
can even act as any app.

7
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.031
So if the help desk holds that role,

8
00:00:20.031 --> 00:00:23.925
the rule that it never approves
consent exists only on paper.

9
00:00:23.925 --> 00:00:25.262
The fix is design.

10
00:00:25.262 --> 00:00:28.407
Every request is triaged
by the authority it needs,

11
00:00:28.407 --> 00:00:33.500
and the help desk gets a custom role that
manages app assignments and nothing else.

12
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.409
The decision for the
identity governance officer:

13
00:00:36.409 --> 00:00:40.287
narrow that role before the help
desk takes its first access ticket.

14
00:00:40.287 --> 00:00:42.260
Next, how apps are tiered.
